views {

  view index {
    title 'System Landscape'
    description 'Big-picture view of the NygDevAzure estate'

    include *

    autoLayout LeftRight 140 120
  }

  view dataflow {
    title 'Data Flow — User, Developer and Azure'
    description '
      One page, read top to bottom: who touches what, what sits behind it,
      and what every box actually runs on. Two people start two very
      different flows.

      A visitor has three entry points — the apex website, the running
      dashboard and the Foundry client. The first and the last lead to the
      RPG Server, where Caddy terminates TLS and hands the request to the
      Foundry process on :30000; the running dashboard leads to the API and
      on to Cosmos DB, one point read per button press. That middle leg is
      drawn site to API, but the browser is what calls: a free-tier Static
      Web App serves files and nothing else, so the page it served reaches
      the function app cross-origin. Foundry asset URLs point at the
      nygdevcdn storage account, and a blob landing there is what wakes the
      admin function.

      The developer pushes to master; the workflows in that same
      repository are what deploy the site into `$web` and the function
      package into func-nygdev-azadmin. Terraform runs from there too, but
      the control plane it goes through is left out here — see `delivery`
      for that, and `oidcTrust` for how a runner gets an Azure token
      without a stored secret.

      One more leg runs outward from the API, to WHOOP, and that one is
      drawn from the API rather than from the browser because it genuinely
      is server-side: the client secret and the refresh token never leave
      Azure, and the vault beside it is where the refresh token lives
      between calls. The visitor edge into the API is the odd one out on
      this page — it is the one-time WHOOP consent, opened by hand, not
      something either site does.

      Two return legs are deliberately left out, because drawing them
      turns a straight read into a loop — Foundry serving the game UI back
      to the client (see `rpg`) and the storage account serving media
      straight to the browser (see `cdn`).
    '

    include
      user,
      dev,
      github,
      github.repo,
      github.actions,
      web,
      running,
      rpg,
      rpg.*,
      cdn,
      azadmin,
      api,
      cosmos,
      keyvault,
      whoop

    exclude
      rpg.foundry -> rpg.client,             // return leg — see `rpg`
      cdn -> user,                           // direct media fetch — see `cdn`
      github.repo.srcWf -> github.actions    // workflows define themselves

    autoLayout TopBottom 130 110

    style * {
      color secondary
    }
    // The two people
    style user, dev {
      color green
    }
    // Runs in the visitor browser
    style web, running, rpg.client {
      color sky
    }
    // Boundaries, not things
    style rpg, github {
      color muted
      opacity 15%
    }
    // Runs on the VM
    style rpg.caddy, rpg.foundry {
      color indigo
    }
    // Build-time only
    style github.repo, github.actions {
      color amber
    }
    // Azure-managed backend
    style cdn, azadmin, api, cosmos, keyvault {
      color primary
    }
    // Outside the estate entirely
    style whoop {
      color muted
    }
  }

  view rpg of rpg {
    title 'RPG Server — Containers'
    description 'Caddy reverse proxy and Foundry VTT on rpg-vm'

    include
      *,
      user,
      cdn,
      cdn -> user

    autoLayout LeftRight 180 100

    style * {
      color secondary
    }
    style rpg {
      opacity 0%
    }
    style rpg._ {
      color primary
    }
    style user {
      color green
    }
  }

  view cdn of cdn {
    title 'CDN Storage'
    description 'Everything that reads from or writes to the nygdevcdn storage account'

    include
      *,
      -> cdn ->

    autoLayout TopBottom 120 130

    style * {
      color secondary
    }
    style cdn {
      color primary
    }
  }

  view whoopSync {
    title 'WHOOP Sync — API, WHOOP and Cosmos DB'
    description '
      The recurring half of the WHOOP integration, and the only part of the
      estate that runs with nobody watching. Read left to right: the API
      opens the vault, spends what it finds there on WHOOP, and writes what
      comes back into Cosmos.

      The vault is on this page because the other two legs do not work
      without it. WHOOP rotates the refresh token on every renewal and kills
      the old one immediately, so the token is not configuration that sits
      in an app setting — it is state the API reads and rewrites on the same
      call, which is why the identity holds Secrets Officer rather than a
      reader role. `whoopRefresh` is that exchange in order.

      What travels the WHOOP leg is four collections — cycles, sleep,
      workouts and recovery — paged 25 records at a time. The first runs
      walk history backwards; once a collection runs out of history every
      later run re-reads the last week instead, because WHOOP filters on
      start time and a record it rescored keeps the start it always had. A
      "since last sync" query would never see it again.

      The Cosmos leg is an upsert keyed on the id WHOOP itself gives a
      record, which is what makes re-reading that week free of duplicates,
      and it carries the cursors too — one document per collection, saying
      how far back the backfill has got. That is the whole reason a run can
      stop on a budget and a later one can pick it up.

      Two triggers drive it and both are the API: /api/whoop/sync on demand,
      and a timer at 07:00 UTC daily. They share one runner and one gate, so
      only ever one of them is walking the cursor. The timer does not call
      the endpoint — there is no HTTP hop between them, and drawing a second
      box for the schedule would suggest one.

      The timer then turns around and reads the workouts back out of Cosmos
      to rebuild the running dashboard, which is why the Cosmos leg runs
      both ways. Where that lands is off this page — a JSON blob on the CDN
      account, drawn on the landscape view. The step is allowed to fail on
      its own: the sync moved a cursor and cannot be repeated, while the
      dashboard is a projection that the next morning rebuilds from scratch
      either way.
    '

    include
      api,
      keyvault,
      whoop,
      cosmos

    autoLayout LeftRight 150 120

    style * {
      color secondary
    }
    // Ours, and inside Azure
    style api, cosmos, keyvault {
      color primary
    }
    // Outside the estate entirely
    style whoop {
      color muted
    }
  }

  dynamic view whoopBootstrap {
    title 'WHOOP consent — the one-time bootstrap'
    description '
      How a refresh token gets into the vault in the first place. It runs
      once, by hand, and only again if the token is revoked or lost — the
      refresh grant can keep a token pair alive forever but cannot create
      one, so a person at the consent screen is the only source.

      Every hop between the API and WHOOP goes through the browser, which
      is why the visitor appears in the middle of a server-side flow: OAuth
      redirects are the browser following Location headers, not two servers
      talking. The client secret and the code exchange never leave Azure.

      The two auth levels differ for a reason. authorize sits behind a
      function key because it is opened by hand. callback cannot: WHOOP
      redirects a browser to it, and a key in a registered redirect URL
      would sit in the developer dashboard and in browser history. What
      guards it instead is the state parameter, signed with the client
      secret and good for ten minutes, so only something that can read the
      vault could have started the flow.
    '

    user -> api 'Opens /api/whoop/authorize (function key)'
    api -> keyvault 'Reads whoop-clientsecret, to sign the state with'
    api -> user '302 to the WHOOP consent screen (client id, scopes, signed state)'
    user -> whoop 'Signs in and grants the scopes'
    whoop -> user '302 back to /api/whoop/callback with a code'
    user -> api 'Browser follows the redirect (anonymous; the state is the gate)'
    api -> whoop 'Exchanges the code (authorization_code grant, client secret)'
    whoop -> api 'Access token, and the first refresh token'
    api -> keyvault 'Writes whoop-token'

    autoLayout TopBottom
  }

  dynamic view whoopRefresh {
    title 'WHOOP refresh — spending and replacing the token'
    description '
      The steady state, once the vault holds a refresh token. No person and
      no browser: the API renews on its own, roughly hourly, and the visitor
      never appears.

      The ordering in the middle is the whole point. WHOOP kills the old
      refresh token the instant it issues a new one, so the reply to that
      one call is the only copy of the replacement that will ever exist.
      It goes to the vault before the access token is used for anything —
      a rotation that succeeds at WHOOP and fails to persist locks the
      integration out until someone re-runs the consent.

      This is also why the API refuses to refresh twice at once, and why it
      re-requests offline every time: without that scope WHOOP returns an
      access token and no replacement, and that refresh would be the last
      one that ever worked.
    '

    api -> keyvault 'Reads whoop-clientsecret and the current whoop-token'
    api -> whoop 'refresh_token grant, re-requesting the offline scope'
    whoop -> api 'New access token, and a rotated refresh token — the old one is now dead'
    api -> keyvault 'Writes the rotated token as a new version of whoop-token'
    api -> whoop 'Calls the data API with the access token (GET /v2/user/profile/basic)'
    whoop -> api 'Returns the data, and the access token is cached until it nears expiry'

    autoLayout TopBottom
  }
}
